From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] Six more live switches (EDEN_UP_LIVE_SWITCHES): usage tracker, query wait once, GPU page cache, unswizzle threads, conditional render on the back, swizzle units

From 11 Nov 2026 a new switch whose effect can change at run time is tested by flipping it
while the game stands at one spot (patch 0478), not by one start per setting. These six are a
plain run-time branch and join the table in common/live_switch.h; each is read again at every
decision it makes, and each decision stands alone:

- EDEN_UP_FRITCHII_USAGE_TRACKER (0513): per Track call. The run of pages known to be all ones
  is reset by Reset whatever the switch says and only grows with it on, so it stays true while
  the plain loop runs. The _VERIFY switch stays boot-fixed and follows the live one.
- EDEN_NX2_QUERY_WAIT_ONCE: per query sync; the recorded copy carries the answer it was recorded
  with. Parsed as before (unset or starting with '0' is off).
- EDEN_UP_GPU_PAGE_CACHE (0541): per lookup, one atomic load. A cached page is checked against
  the mapping serial at every use.
- EDEN_UP_UNSWIZZLE_THREADS=<0-4> (0542): per large unswizzle. Helper threads are now made the
  first time a value asks for them (none at boot) and stay; a smaller value uses the first n of
  them, the others skip that run. Checked off the console under the thread sanitizer: 13,940
  runs from two callers with the count changing at random between 0 and 4, every line done
  exactly once, no race, no hang.
- EDEN_UP_COND_BACK (0543; "1" on, "2" verify): per run of the conditional render macro, which
  sets the back's condition in full either way.
- EDEN_UP_SWIZZLE_UNITS (0544): per copy; two ways to copy the same bytes.

Without EDEN_UP_LIVE_SWITCHES=1 every one of them is still what the environment gave at its first
use, for ever: nothing changes for a batch or for play.

NOT made live, on purpose: EDEN_UP_BACK_DRAW_CHEAP keeps an alias tick per image only while it
is on (SetModificationTick), so turning it on in mid-run would skip alias syncs that are due; a
live form needs that bookkeeping always on and edits in five files, two of which the PS5 front
rewrites at build time. It stays a batch. So do the boot-fixed kinds: anything that changes
shader translation or the driver (EDEN_NX2_RADV_*), heap and thread layout, clean against fast.

